hi there hi there this is Steve Kaufman  I'm going to talk a little bit about  something that relates to language and  it relates to education but it's a  little bit different from the straight  language learning and I've been talking  about and it relates to what is known as  critical thinking and I have been on  this email exchange listserv forum where  people who are involved in ESL teaching  and helping immigrants and so forth have  been discussing what they call I think  there were several subjects one was  critical thinking beginning ESL class um  I think they were talking a little bit  about the use of technology in the  classroom I guess got into a lot of  different subjects but the critical  thinking I have the impression that  people who teach language to immigrants  or low literacy learners think that they  are going to teach them how to think and  I have been struggling with this and I  just cannot see how you do that I think  that you can help people reason you can  help people first of all you have to  help them improve their language if  they're gonna speak intelligently in a  language need a lot of words so the  first part of it before you even worry  about helping them learn how to think to  my mind has to be to get them to acquire  a lot of words I mean it depends what  they're gonna talk about if they're  gonna talk about things that are very  basic and simple then they don't need a  lot of words but again it depends on  their goals but I think words getting  some natural expressions in the language  is number one you shouldn't mess with  their thinking at that stage and then  you can help  with some formulas for organizing their  thoughts so that they become a little  more persuasive at least persuasive in  terms of the culture of the language  you're teaching so that in the case of  English we would suggest to them that  they express their views this way that  they support their arguments this way  because that's more persuasive to an  english-speaking listener I suppose if  you are learning Spanish or Chinese then  there would be arranged a different  range of ways of expressing your views  that that are considered persuasive and  so you really learn how to apply some  form of logic to justify the position  that you have because most people arrive  at a position on certain subjects based  more on emotion then on logic few people  sit back and sort of have a blank slate  and say now what do I really think about  you know whatever global warming  starting from nothing from no influence  from family from anything just like I'm  gonna deduce a position you know that  just doesn't happen and the proof is  that that people you know very  well-educated people at least in terms  of our book learning type of education  it can have tremendously different views  on something so and which is a good  thing  I mean people should have different  views that means that they have all used  logic to defend whatever position they  arrived at and the more logical you are  the better you are using arguments and  appearing to analyze and synthesize and  investigate and observe and perhaps look  at two sides of the coin  all of this makes you more persuasive  but if the idea and I on this email  exchange that I'm on it's a lot of  teachers and they're all talking about  wonderful projects that they've been on  and encouraging their learners to be  open minded and question their role in  society and who has  our and society and and of course really  what they're doing is they're forcing  their sort of activist agenda whether it  be women's issues or the power of the  powerless or whatever they're trying to  kind of inculcate these activist views  into their students who really only want  to learn English and the funding for  these programs is for learning English  but these teachers don't think that  stimulating enough so they want to teach  them to stand up for their human rights  however define but if it comes to a  question of being open-minded and  willing to really evaluate one's  positions I must say that I don't find  educated people such as university  people I don't find educated people any  more open-minded than people with less  education I find that in many cases that  less educated people are more willing to  listen they maybe they have less you  know hard and fast views on things so  that for a number of things they at  least perhaps they're more humble  they're prepared to listen many  University certainly people who work at  universities are people like lawyers and  people like that they do not listen at  all and you have in the same category  I'd like to think that I listen and I  like to think that I I apply some form  of analysis to the arguments that are  presented to me but I'm sure that I'm  actually quite wedded to my prejudices  and but not as badly as some and and  when I think of some of the tremendously  stupid things that university professors  say at conferences on issues that I know  something about I don't think there are  powers of critical thinking are any  better than anyone elses so I don't  think you can teach critical thinking I  think people think the way they do they  think more with their emotions and some  people have better skills at developing  arguments to support the position  reached largely emotionally and and the  idea that you can teach critical  thinking in a classroom and in  particular to beginning ESL people I  just think is a pipe dream I think that  as you know my view on language learning  is all those beginning ESL students  learners would be better off to have  stories that they can listen to and read  and listen to over and over again and  don't ask them in class you know how  what was what is the reason that you  left your little village as a refugee  and came to North America and who has  power in the society here and what is  your role and and all of this kind of  thing asking them to question their role  in society and why they 